= Unit 1 · Binding and substitution

== Lecture 1 · Lambda terms and free variables
#focus[Parse lambda terms, distinguish binding from application, and compute free variables. Prerequisites: inductive definitions and elementary logic.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall inductively generated trees and that one variable name may occur both freely and bound in different subterms.


The untyped lambda calculus isolates two operations: constructing a function and applying it. Terms are generated by variables, application $t u$, and abstraction $lambda x.t$. Application associates to the left, so $t u v$ means $(t u)v$. An abstraction's body extends as far right as parentheses permit. Thus $lambda x.x y$ is a function with body $x y$.

#definition[Free variables][Define $FV(x)=\{x\}$, $FV(t u)=FV(t)union FV(u)$, and $FV(lambda x.t)=FV(t)without\{x\}$. A term is closed if it has no free variables. An occurrence of $x$ is bound by the nearest enclosing abstraction naming $x$, if one exists.]

#example[Shadowing][In $lambda x.(x(lambda x.x))y$, the first occurrence of $x$ is bound by the outer abstraction and the occurrence inside $lambda x.x$ by the inner one. The variable $y$ is free. Renaming the inner binder first makes the structure clearer: $lambda x.(x(lambda z.z))y$.]

An inductive definition of terms gives a structural induction principle: prove a property for every variable, prove it is inherited by an application from its two subterms, and prove it is inherited by an abstraction from its body. This is different from induction on a reduction sequence, which will concern computations rather than syntax trees.

#definition[Alpha equivalence][Renaming a bound variable consistently to a fresh name does not change the binding structure. Alpha equivalence is the equivalence generated by such renamings and their closure under term formation. For example $lambda x.x$ and $lambda z.z$ are alpha equivalent, but $lambda x.y$ and $lambda y.y$ are not.]

#strategy[Use structural induction and keep the binding relation invariant under fresh renaming.]

#theorem[Free variables respect alpha equivalence][Alpha-equivalent terms have the same free variables.]
#proof[It suffices to check one capture-free renaming and its placement inside a larger term. In a renamed abstraction, occurrences bound by that abstraction change name, and the new name is chosen fresh for the body; none becomes free and no previously free occurrence becomes bound. Thus removing the bound variable from the body's free-variable set gives the same result. The application and abstraction context cases follow from the recursive equations for $FV$. Reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity preserve equality of free-variable sets.]

Named variables are readable but require freshness management. An alternative is de Bruijn indexing: a bound variable records how many binders outward one must go to find its binder. This removes alpha-renaming choices but requires careful index shifts under binders. We retain names in the mathematical development and make freshness conditions explicit.

#example[Reading a term before manipulating it][In $(lambda x.x y)(lambda y.y)$, the left abstraction binds only its occurrence of $x$. Its $y$ is free. The right abstraction binds a different occurrence of the name $y$. The entire term therefore has free-variable set $\{y\}$. Renaming the right binder to $z$ makes this separation visible without changing the term's alpha class.]

To calculate, mark each abstraction's body first, then connect each bound occurrence to its nearest matching binder. Only after those connections are clear should a substitution be attempted. A string search for a letter cannot distinguish these scopes.

#checkpoint("1.1", [Compute $FV(lambda x.(x z)(lambda z.y z))$.], [It is $\{y,z\}$: the first $z$ is free, while the second abstraction binds its own occurrences of $z$.])
#takeaways[Read the syntax tree before performing a substitution or reduction. Identical variable names can denote different bound occurrences.]
