== Lecture 2 · Capture-avoiding substitution
#focus[Define substitution recursively, prove its free-variable bound, and apply the substitution-composition law with its freshness condition.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall the free-variable set and the unchanged-term lemma when the replaced variable is absent.


#definition[Substitution][$t[x:=u]$ replaces the free occurrences of $x$ in $t$ by $u$. For variables, $x[x:=u]=u$ and $y[x:=u]=y$ when $y!=x$. Substitution distributes over applications. Under $lambda x$, it stops. Under $lambda y$ with $y!=x$, first ensure $y notin FV(u)$ by alpha-renaming if necessary, then substitute in the body.]

#example[Why renaming is necessary][In $(lambda y.x)[x:=y]$, the inserted $y$ must remain free. Rename the binder to fresh $z$ first, obtaining $lambda z.y$. The expression $lambda y.y$ would incorrectly capture the inserted variable and turn a constant function into the identity.]

#strategy[Induct on syntax, renaming each binder fresh before passing substitution underneath it.]

#theorem[Free-variable bound][$FV(t[x:=u]) subset.eq (FV(t)without\{x\})union FV(u)$. If $x notin FV(t)$, substitution leaves $t$ unchanged up to alpha equivalence.]
#proof[Induct on $t$. For a variable, use the two defining cases. For application, use the induction bounds on its two parts and take their union. For an abstraction binding $x$, substitution stops and no free $x$ occurs in the abstraction. For another binder $y$, choose it fresh for $u$; apply the induction hypothesis to its body, then remove $y$ from the free-variable set. Freshness ensures this removal does not delete a free variable newly inserted from $u$. The unchanged-term assertion follows from the same induction.]

#theorem[Substitution composition][If $x!=y$ and $x notin FV(v)$, then, up to alpha equivalence,
$ (t[x:=u])[y:=v] = (t[y:=v])[x:=u[y:=v]]. $
All bound names are chosen fresh for the terms being substituted.]
#proof[Induct on $t$. If $t=x$, both sides are $u[y:=v]$. If $t=y$, the left side is $v$, while the right is $v[x:=u[y:=v]]=v$ by the freshness assumption. Any other variable is unchanged. The application case follows componentwise. For an abstraction, first rename its binder to avoid $x,y$ and all free variables in $u,v$; substitution then passes under it on both sides, and the induction hypothesis proves equality of the bodies.]

The freshness assumption is essential. With $t=y$ and $v=x$, the left side gives $x$ while the right side may substitute inside that newly inserted $x$. Composition laws are about controlled substitution, not arbitrary textual replacement.

#example[Composition records which occurrence was inserted][Take distinct variables $x,y,z,w$, with $t=x y$, $u=y z$, and $v=w$. Substituting $u$ for $x$ and then $v$ for $y$ gives $(w z)w$. Reversing the two steps naively would give $(y z)w$. The correct composition law also substitutes $v$ into the replacement $u$, giving $w z$ before it is inserted.]

The equation is therefore more than a convenient rearrangement of notation: it tracks whether a later substitution should act inside an earlier replacement. Its side condition prevents an occurrence inserted by the second substitution from being changed incorrectly on the other side.

#checkpoint("1.2", [Compute $(lambda y.x y)[x:=y]$.], [Rename the bound $y$ to fresh $z$, then substitute, giving $lambda z.y z$.])
#takeaways[Substitution is the operation behind both computation and dependent typing. Establish capture avoidance now; later metatheorems rely on it at every binder.]
